1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,420
Los Angeles, 1965.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,760
A man steps into a glass phone

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:05,076
booth on a busy sidewalk,

4
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:07,032
pulls the door shut behind him,

5
00:00:07,180 --> 00:00:08,391
and drops in his coins.

6
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:10,343
He believes he is alone.

7
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:11,760
He is wrong.

8
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:13,730
Taped to the top of that booth,

9
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:16,160
on the outside, is a small microphone

10
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:17,367
the FBI put there —

11
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:19,927
quietly recording every word he says.

12
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,192
The agents never open the door.

13
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:24,161
They never set foot inside.

14
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:26,232
They never lay a finger on him.

15
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:28,380
Two years later, the Supreme Court will

16
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:29,780
look at what those agents did

17
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:31,995
and call it an illegal search —

18
00:00:31,996 --> 00:00:33,700
and in the same stroke tear

19
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:35,280
up a rule that had stood

20
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,521
for nearly forty years.

21
00:00:37,522 --> 00:00:41,747
This is the case that decided the Constitution
protects people, not places.

22
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:43,490
For most of American history,

23
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:45,941
the Fourth Amendment was
about physical space.

24
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:47,800
Your home, your papers,

25
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:49,160
your property —

26
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:51,040
the government could not come

27
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:54,840
in and search them without a warrant. And
the rule was nearly that literal:

28
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:57,580
if the police did not physically intrude

29
00:00:57,580 --> 00:00:58,791
into a protected place,

30
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:01,590
there was no search at all.

31
00:01:01,591 --> 00:01:03,539
But a phone call breaks that picture.

32
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:05,860
Your most private words leave your mouth

33
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:07,140
and travel down a wire,

34
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:09,000
out of any room you could ever

35
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:09,900
lock.

36
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:11,660
So what protects them?

37
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:14,066
Over the next twelve minutes:

38
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:16,110
the hidden microphone that started it,

39
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:17,200
the old rule it broke,

40
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,340
and the single sentence that moved

41
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:22,200
the Constitution from guarding
places to guarding

42
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:23,185
people.

43
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:25,580
The man in the booth was named Charles Katz.

44
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:27,880
In Los Angeles, in 1965,

45
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:30,195
he was running a betting operation —

46
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:32,640
taking wagers and passing
gambling information

47
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:33,540
over the phone.

48
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:35,100
To keep it quiet,

49
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:36,736
he did the careful thing.

50
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:38,380
He did not make those calls

51
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:39,280
from home.

52
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:40,820
He walked to a row of public

53
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:43,619
phone booths on the street and
placed them from there.

54
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:45,520
The FBI had come to suspect him,

55
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:49,031
and the agents wanted proof
in his own voice —

56
00:01:49,032 --> 00:01:50,817
Katz placing the bets himself.

57
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:52,519
The trouble was how to get it.

58
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:55,170
They could not squeeze into
the booth beside him.

59
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:56,920
So they tried something the law had

60
00:01:56,920 --> 00:01:58,078
never squarely tested.

61
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:00,194
They waited until Katz left,

62
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:04,700
and then they went to work on the booth
itself. On the outside surface,

63
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:07,000
up on top, they fixed a small

64
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:08,334
electronic device —

65
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:10,614
a microphone wired to a recorder.

66
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:12,640
When Katz came back and placed

67
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:14,840
his calls, the machine caught his end

68
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:16,980
of every conversation while the agents sat

69
00:02:16,980 --> 00:02:18,429
nearby and listened.

70
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:19,981
They never opened the door.

71
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:21,548
They never reached inside.

72
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,540
Everything they took, they took off

73
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:25,268
the outside of the glass.

74
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:26,240
It worked.

75
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:29,180
The tapes caught Katz passing
wagering information

76
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:31,580
across state lines, to bookmakers in Miami

77
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:32,480
and Boston.

78
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:35,170
He was charged under a federal gambling law.

79
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:39,635
At trial, the government played the recordings
against him, and he was convicted.

80
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:42,080
Katz appealed, and the fight narrowed

81
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:43,800
to one strange question.

82
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:45,851
Not whether he did it —

83
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:47,860
the tapes were clear on that.

84
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:52,530
The question was whether the government should
ever have been allowed to make them .

85
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:53,920
To see why that was hard,

86
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:58,080
you have to know the rule the courts were
living by. For nearly forty years,

87
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:59,700
the Fourth Amendment had been tied

88
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:01,357
to physical trespass.

89
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:03,840
The rule came from a 1928 case

90
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:04,740
called Olmstead.

91
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:09,740
Federal agents there had tapped a bootlegger's
phone lines, but they did the tapping

92
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:11,140
from wires out on the street,

93
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:12,960
without ever entering his house.

94
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:15,587
The Supreme Court said that was fine:

95
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:18,380
no trespass into a protected place meant

96
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:19,701
no search.

97
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:22,234
For decades, the test stayed
almost mechanical.

98
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:24,620
Did the government physically intrude?

99
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:25,710
If it did not,

100
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:27,856
the Fourth Amendment simply did not apply

101
00:03:27,886 --> 00:03:28,370
.

102
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:30,260
By that rule, the agents watching Katz

103
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:31,471
had done nothing wrong.

104
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:33,306
They never entered the booth.

105
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:34,781
They never broke the glass.

106
00:03:34,980 --> 00:03:36,660
They fixed a device to the outside

107
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:38,830
and gathered sound out of the open air.

108
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:40,191
No trespass —

109
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:41,836
and so, by the old logic,

110
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:43,471
no search at all.

111
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:44,580
But push on that,

112
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:46,294
and it starts to feel wrong.

113
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:49,460
Katz had done everything a person
can do to be private.

114
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:50,747
He stepped inside.

115
00:03:51,220 --> 00:03:52,120
He shut the door.

116
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:53,525
He paid for the call.

117
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:55,700
The whole purpose of that little glass

118
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:57,480
room is to have a conversation

119
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:00,580
the sidewalk cannot hear.

120
00:04:00,581 --> 00:04:03,956
If the government can record you there simply
because it never touched the walls,

121
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:06,660
then your privacy hangs on a technicality

122
00:04:07,516 --> 00:04:07,816
—

123
00:04:07,817 --> 00:04:11,763
on where a microphone sits, not on
what you reasonably expected.

124
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:13,880
And tying privacy to places carried

125
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:14,780
a stranger flaw.

126
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:16,892
A phone booth is made of glass.

127
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:20,140
Anyone walking past can see
you standing inside.

128
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:22,100
If Fourth Amendment protection depended

129
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:23,468
on being hidden from view,

130
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:26,360
a see-through booth would
protect almost nothing.

131
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:28,800
Yet what Katz wanted to keep private

132
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:30,063
was never how he looked.

133
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:31,847
It was what he said.

134
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:33,140
So which was it?

135
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:37,728
A clever piece of surveillance that broke
no rule because nobody trespassed —

136
00:04:37,729 --> 00:04:39,700
or a search of a private conversation,

137
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:41,953
dressed up as something else?

138
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:44,160
On December 18, 1967,

139
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:46,422
the Supreme Court gave its answer —

140
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:48,030
seven justices to one.

141
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:50,768
The opinion came from Justice
Potter Stewart,

142
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:52,800
and it did not merely decide Katz's

143
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:53,670
case.

144
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:55,569
It rewrote the ground rules.

145
00:04:55,940 --> 00:04:57,700
Stewart began by throwing out the old

146
00:04:57,700 --> 00:04:58,600
question.

147
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:01,023
The government kept arguing about the booth

148
00:05:01,425 --> 00:05:01,725
—

149
00:05:01,726 --> 00:05:03,042
was it a protected place,

150
00:05:03,180 --> 00:05:04,496
was it public or private?

151
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:06,680
Wrong frame, the Court said.

152
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:09,240
"The Fourth Amendment protects people,

153
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:12,800
not places." What a person knowingly exposes

154
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:14,720
to the public, even in his own

155
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:16,380
home, is not protected.

156
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:18,790
But what he tries to keep private,

157
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:20,670
even somewhere the public can see him,

158
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:21,690
can be.

159
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:23,782
Then came the line that decided it.

160
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:25,220
A man who steps into a phone

161
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:26,957
booth, shuts the door behind him,

162
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:29,940
and pays the toll to place his
call is entitled to assume

163
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:33,260
that the words he speaks into the
mouthpiece will not be broadcast

164
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:36,560
to the world.

165
00:05:36,561 --> 00:05:39,881
Katz had not shut that door
to hide his face —

166
00:05:39,882 --> 00:05:42,757
the glass made that impossible. He shut
it to keep his words private.

167
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:44,660
And that expectation, the Court held,

168
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:47,427
is one the Constitution protects.

169
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:50,170
With that, the Court retired
the trespass rule.

170
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:53,913
It no longer mattered that the agents had
never physically entered the booth.

171
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:55,788
The old Olmstead doctrine,

172
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:57,740
the Court said, had been worn away

173
00:05:57,740 --> 00:05:59,980
by later decisions and could no longer

174
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:01,579
be regarded as controlling.

175
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:04,100
A search did not require a break-in.

176
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:08,039
It could happen through a microphone taped
to the outside of a glass door

177
00:06:08,183 --> 00:06:08,630
.

178
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:10,871
But notice exactly what the Court faulted.

179
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:13,420
The agents had real reason to suspect

180
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:14,320
Katz —

181
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:15,820
they had probable cause.

182
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:20,060
They had even limited their recording to
the moments he was actually placing

183
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:20,960
calls.

184
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:22,640
All of that, the Court said,

185
00:06:23,020 --> 00:06:24,020
was to their credit,

186
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:25,180
and beside the point.

187
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:28,200
Their mistake was acting
on their own authority.

188
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:31,980
They never carried their evidence to
a judge and asked permission before

189
00:06:31,980 --> 00:06:34,046
they began to listen.

190
00:06:34,047 --> 00:06:35,960
A search run without that prior approval

191
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,260
from a neutral magistrate,

192
00:06:37,260 --> 00:06:39,540
the Court held, is unreasonable almost

193
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:40,864
by definition.

194
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:42,660
One justice was not persuaded.

195
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:45,040
Justice Hugo Black dissented,

196
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:45,940
alone.

197
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:49,027
His argument came down to the
plain words on the page.

198
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:51,427
The Fourth Amendment, he pointed out,

199
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:53,800
protects "persons, houses, papers,

200
00:06:53,940 --> 00:06:56,098
and effects" against search and seizure —

201
00:06:56,680 --> 00:06:58,885
tangible things you can point
to and carry away.

202
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,553
A spoken conversation is none of those.

203
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:03,454
You cannot seize a sentence.

204
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:06,780
To Black, the majority was not so
much reading the Constitution

205
00:07:06,780 --> 00:07:08,988
as quietly rewriting it —

206
00:07:09,060 --> 00:07:10,955
and rewriting it was Congress's job,

207
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:12,597
not the Court's.

208
00:07:12,660 --> 00:07:14,700
It is worth being precise about what

209
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:16,240
Katz did, and what it did

210
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:17,140
not do.

211
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:19,140
It did not ban wiretapping.

212
00:07:19,140 --> 00:07:20,940
It did not tell the government

213
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:23,735
it could never record a suspect.

214
00:07:23,736 --> 00:07:27,200
The Court's message was closer to
the opposite: go get a warrant.

215
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:29,180
Take your suspicion to a judge,

216
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:30,605
lay out your reasons,

217
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:31,956
and get permission first.

218
00:07:32,340 --> 00:07:34,280
Do that, and surveillance like this can

219
00:07:34,280 --> 00:07:35,812
be entirely lawful.

220
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:37,260
And the most famous part

221
00:07:37,260 --> 00:07:38,380
of the case is not even

222
00:07:38,380 --> 00:07:39,433
in the main opinion.

223
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:41,400
It sits in a short concurrence

224
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:45,449
by Justice John Marshall Harlan.

225
00:07:45,450 --> 00:07:48,460
Trying to turn the majority's reasoning into
a rule other courts could actually

226
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:50,380
apply, Harlan laid out a two-part test.

227
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:53,340
First, a person has to genuinely expect

228
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:54,240
privacy.

229
00:07:54,740 --> 00:07:56,760
Second, that expectation has to be one

230
00:07:57,660 --> 00:07:59,923
society is prepared to accept as reasonable

231
00:07:59,955 --> 00:08:00,370
.

232
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:01,642
Harlan's phrase —

233
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,053
a "reasonable expectation of privacy" —

234
00:08:04,540 --> 00:08:07,220
was only one justice's gloss in 1967.

235
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:09,752
But in the years that followed,

236
00:08:09,940 --> 00:08:11,700
courts reached for it again and again,

237
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:13,740
until it became the standard test

238
00:08:13,740 --> 00:08:15,480
for what even counts as a search

239
00:08:15,481 --> 00:08:16,150
.

240
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:18,100
When judges today ask whether the police

241
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:19,942
need a warrant to track your phone,

242
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:22,273
read your messages, or follow your car,

243
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:25,171
they are usually asking Harlan's question.

244
00:08:25,420 --> 00:08:28,120
And that is both the strength
of Katz and its soft spot.

245
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:31,280
By anchoring privacy to what society thinks

246
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:33,480
is reasonable, the Court made the Fourth

247
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:36,164
Amendment flexible enough to
follow new technology.

248
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:38,800
But "reasonable" is a moving target.

249
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:40,740
The more ordinary it becomes

250
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:42,840
to be watched, the weaker anyone's claim

251
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:45,002
that a given expectation still holds.

252
00:08:45,660 --> 00:08:47,353
Katz freed privacy from the walls —

253
00:08:47,354 --> 00:08:48,880
and tied it, instead,

254
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:50,500
to what we are still willing

255
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:51,718
to expect.

256
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:53,080
So go back to that man

257
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:54,080
in the glass booth,

258
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:56,520
dropping his coins, pulling the door shut,

259
00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:59,598
certain he was alone.

260
00:08:59,599 --> 00:09:03,680
The agents outside never touched him, never
stepped in, never broke a single

261
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:04,734
thing.

262
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:05,780
Under the old rule,

263
00:09:05,900 --> 00:09:08,159
that was the perfect trick —

264
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:10,740
surveillance with no trespass,
and therefore no search.

265
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:12,700
The Supreme Court looked

266
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:13,800
at it and said no.

267
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:17,220
The Fourth Amendment had stopped
guarding only walls and doors.

268
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:19,700
It now guarded the person standing inside

269
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:20,600
them.

270
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:22,720
What made that possible was a small

271
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:23,878
shift in the question.

272
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:27,181
Stop asking where the government
was standing;

273
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:29,920
start asking what the person
reasonably expected.

274
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:33,320
That single move let the Constitution keep

275
00:09:33,320 --> 00:09:34,360
pace with a wire,

276
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:37,220
a microphone, and every listening device

277
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:38,120
that came after.

278
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:40,460
But eavesdropping is only one way

279
00:09:40,460 --> 00:09:42,302
the state can reach into your life.

280
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:45,284
Sometimes it does not listen —

281
00:09:45,285 --> 00:09:46,310
it simply stops you.

282
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:49,464
Picture a car pulled to the shoulder
of a dark highway near midnight.

283
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:52,420
A routine ticket, the business already done.

284
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:56,796
Then the officer holds you there a little
longer, waiting for a drug-sniffing

285
00:09:56,980 --> 00:09:57,880
dog to arrive.

286
00:09:58,520 --> 00:09:59,420
No new suspicion.

287
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:00,800
Just time.

288
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:02,920
How long can the police keep you,

289
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:04,400
once the reason for the stop

290
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:05,300
is over?

291
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:08,870
That is a different corner of
the same Constitution —

292
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:10,278
and that is next time.
